\section*{The mass and mean length of critical bridges in a fixed-width corridor}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The linked manuscripts present the following claims by OpenAI: The critical honeycomb strip-crossing mass is claimed comparable to $N^{-1/4}$, and the displacement-weighted return mass to $N^{3/4}$; After summing heights through order $h(\log h)^{1/64}$, a logarithmic window around $h^{4/3}$ is claimed to carry at least $h^{3/4}(\log h)^{-C}$ bridge mass; Critically weighted all-length bridges with a fixed starting port are claimed to have mean length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$ even under confinement, with mass of order $R^{-1/4}$; Polynomial-vacuum calculations are claimed to give mean bridge length $h^{4/3+o(1)}$, central chord-visit exponent $-2/3$, and chord mean exponent $4/3$; The proposed spatial exponent is $3/4$ for infinite irreducible bridges, all compatible large even fixed-length bridges, and thermal laws; unrestricted endpoint lower bounds hold on one density-one set of lengths; Uniform two-mark polygon control is claimed to imply bridge mass $h^{-1/4}$ and finite length-mass bounds, with a definite portion on lengths at least order $h^{4/3}$. Together they give a claimed affirmative answer in the matching scope.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} In a honeycomb strip of \(N\) triangular bands, weight self-avoiding port paths by \(\rho^{|\gamma|}\), \(\rho=(2+\sqrt2)^{-1/2}\). From a fixed bottom source, let \(\mathcal A_N\) be total return-arch mass, \(\mathcal B_N\) total top-bridge mass, \(K_N(k)\) the mass of arches ending at bottom port \(k\), and \(m_N=\sum_{k\ge1}kK_N(k)\). The claim gives finite sums and
\[
 \cos(3\pi/8)\mathcal A_N+\mathcal B_N=1,\quad
 m_{N+1}-m_N\asymp\mathcal B_N,\quad
 m_N\asymp N^{3/4},\quad\mathcal B_N\asymp N^{-1/4},
\]
with \(\mathcal B_N\) nonincreasing. Here \(m_N\) is a horizontal-displacement moment, not a bridge mean length. Length counts visited honeycomb vertices, equivalently triangle centres; terminal half-edges contribute no weight. An arch or bridge has no boundary contact beyond its two endpoints.

\paragraph{Result 2.} At honeycomb critical fugacity \(X=(2+\sqrt2)^{-1/2}\), put \(\mu(\omega)=X^{|\omega|}\), counting visited vertices. For large real \(h\), define \(R_h=h(\log h)^{1/64}\), \(\ell_-=h^{4/3}(\log h)^{-1/8}\), \(\ell_+=h^{4/3}(\log h)^{1/2}\). The claim gives
\[
 \sum_{1\le H\le2R_h}\sum_{\omega\in\mathcal B_H,\ \ell_-\le|\omega|\le2\ell_+}\mu(\omega)
 \ge c h^{3/4}(\log h)^{-C}.
\]
This averages over integer bridge heights. Separate finite estimates are \(B_H\asymp H^{-1/4}\), arch mass \(A_g\asymp g^{-5/4}\), with a lower bound retained at diameter \(O(g)\). A fixed-width \(TH\) bridge corridor retains mass \(c_TH^{-1/4}\) and has length-weighted mass at most \(C_TH^{13/12}\), even after fixing either weak endpoint-displacement sign. For strip arches/bridges, diameter tail mass is at most \(CH^{-1/4}e^{-cr/H}\) for \(r\ge CH\). Polygon shells satisfy \(\sum_{R\le\operatorname{diam}P<2R}\mu(P)|P|^2\le CR^{2/3}(1+\log\log R)\). In convex finite triangle domains of diameter \(C_0r\) and bounded side count, all-endpoint chord length-weighted mass is at most \(Cr^{25/12}\). The polygon sum ranges over unrooted polygons modulo primitive lattice translations, so each translation class is counted once.

\paragraph{Result 3.} Between honeycomb walls separated by admissible distance $R\in(3/2)\mathbb Z_{>0}$, fix a source port and weight each self-avoiding bridge by \(x^{\ell(\gamma)}\), \(x=(2+\sqrt2)^{-1/2}\), counting vertices. Let \(B(R)\) and \(M(R)\) be bridge mass and length-weighted mass, and \(B_C,M_C\) their restrictions to lateral displacement at most \(CR\) throughout the path. The source claims some \(C_0\) such that every fixed \(C\ge C_0\) has
\[
 B_C(R)\asymp B(R)\asymp R^{-1/4},\qquad
 M_C(R)=R^{13/12+o(1)},\quad M(R)=R^{13/12+o(1)}.
\]
Both normalized mean vertex counts are therefore \(R^{4/3+o(1)}\).

\paragraph{Result 4.} For critical honeycomb self-avoiding port bridges across \(h\) triangular layers, let \(b_h\) sum weights \(x_*^{|\gamma|}\) over all top endpoints and \(\ell_h\) sum \(|\gamma|x_*^{|\gamma|}\), with \(x_*=(2+\sqrt2)^{-1/2}\). The source asserts finite sums and
\[
 b_h=h^{-1/4+o(1)},\qquad\ell_h=h^{13/12+o(1)},\qquad
 \mathbb E_h|\gamma|=h^{4/3+o(1)}.
\]
Restricting every path to horizontal distance \(h\log^2h\) from its source preserves all three exponents. Length is visited-vertex count.

\paragraph{Result 5.} Concatenate independent critical irreducible honeycomb bridges from the fixed source, with length \(L_k\), height \(H_k\), and cumulative height \(S_H(k)\). The source's weaker-finite-input corollary claims, for the \(n\)-th vertex and its height \(Y_n\), almost surely
\[
 Y_n\ge n^{3/4-o(1)},\qquad|\gamma_n-\gamma_0|\le n^{17/18+o(1)}.
\]
Sum the finite bridge measure across all heights using weights \(\rho^Le^{-L/n}\). The resulting law has height at least \(n^{3/4-\varepsilon}\) with probability tending to one. Long half-plane arch mass is at most \(n^{-3/16+o(1)}\). For \(V_h=\sum_kL_k\mathbf1_{S_H(k)\le h}\) and its version counting only completed prefixes confined laterally to \(Ch\log^2h\), both expected rewards are \(h^{4/3+o(1)}\). Consequently the expected vertex count before first disk exit is at least \(R^{4/3-o(1)}\). Here $\rho=(2+\sqrt2)^{-1/2}$, and the independent irreducible pieces have probability $p(\eta)=\rho^{L(\eta)}$. A bridge is cut into these pieces at horizontal lines which the path crosses exactly once.

\paragraph{Result 6.} Let \(\mathcal B_h\) be honeycomb port bridges of height \(h\), critical weights \(w(\gamma)=(2+\sqrt2)^{-\ell(\gamma)/2}\), and total mass \(B_h\). The claimed constants \(c,C>0\) satisfy, for sufficiently large \(h\),
\[
 ch^{-1/4}\le B_h\le Ch^{-1/4},\qquad
 \sum_{\gamma\in\mathcal B_h}\ell(\gamma)w(\gamma)\le Ch^{13/12},
\]
\[
 \sum_{\gamma\in\mathcal B_h:\ell(\gamma)\ge ch^{4/3}}w(\gamma)\ge ch^{-1/4}.
\]
For all \(h\ge1,x\ge0\), $\sum_{\gamma\in\mathcal B_h:\operatorname{diam}\gamma>x}w(\gamma)\le C(1+x)^{-1/4}$. The integer $h$ counts triangular layers, with physical strip width $h\sqrt3/2$. The source port is fixed and the opposite endpoint is summed freely; $\ell(\gamma)$ counts visited centres.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Positive strip flux is represented by finite transfer rows and a polynomial vacuum. An exact arch-moment formula becomes a positive homogeneous integral; isolating its smallest coordinate determines the strip exponent. Finite-circumference loop expansions are recalibrated against positive cap amplitudes. Winding-pressure tails and divided-slice enclosing estimates transfer this normalization to strip crossings, concentrating a controlled mass in the specified length window after height averaging.

Local exploration yields a disk transfer representation and an inverse-matrix identity along a rapidity orbit. A boundary deficit and two-gap cylinder contour calculation fix the normalization; two-bridge and winding-polygon bounds then control confinement and mean length. Row matrices construct the balanced puncture polynomial and its boundary asymptotics. Surrounding-polygon localization transports the cylinder observable into finite chord visits, while a marked-tripod comparison converts those visits into the strip bridge mean.

All laws are represented on a common renewal space. Exact-length and logarithmic-window conditioning are combined with recoverable geometric cuts, and controlled changes of measure extend the bounds to thermal and unrestricted ensembles; localized endpoint estimates also yield pulling and correlation-scale exponents. Positive interpolation gives the strip exponent, and a periodic vacuum pairing fixes physical normalization. Controlled-current contour expansions and cylinder barriers produce nesting and unequal-mark bounds; these retain the marked-separation power needed for finite bridge moments.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The strip theorem supplies finite critical masses comparable to R\textasciicircum{}(-1/4) and return-displacement moments. The fixed-corridor first length mass and both R\textasciicircum{}(4/3) mean-length statements are outside it. The finite boundary theorem proves B\_H comparable to H\textasciicircum{}(-1/4), retains mass at least c\_T H\textasciicircum{}(-1/4) inside every sufficiently large fixed transverse corridor TH, and bounds that confined first length mass above by C\_T H\textasciicircum{}(13/12). Together with the unrestricted upper mass bound this settles both mass comparisons and gives a confined normalized-mean upper bound C\_T H\textasciicircum{}(4/3). It does not prove a matching fixed-height first-moment lower bound, either confined or unconfined, nor both R\textasciicircum{}(13/12+o(1)) first moments. The main logarithmic-window lower bound is summed over heights and cannot be substituted for those fixed-height all-length claims. This manuscript has no individually listed Lean scope, so the main Lean flag is null. Critically weighted all-length bridges with a fixed starting port are claimed to have mean length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$ even under confinement, with mass of order $R^{-1/4}$. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. The source proves the unconfined exponent laws and confinement only to width R(log R)\textasciicircum{}2. It does not retain them in every sufficiently wide fixed-aspect corridor. The renewal/change-of-law theorem treats concatenated infinite bridges and finite height laws, with R(log R)\textasciicircum{}2 confinement. It does not supply the target fixed-aspect finite-corridor mass and first-moment comparisons. The source gives finite bridge mass and sharp first-moment/long-fraction estimates in the unconfined bridge law, without the fixed-aspect confined law. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 237, the selected Lean statements give critical-strip mass and displacement exponents, bridge summability, and existence of pulled free energy. The $4/3$ bridge-length and $3/4$ spatial laws and the other polygon/chord exponents are outside these statements. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{CriticalStripMass.lean}, \texttt{HoneycombBridgeFiniteness.lean}, \texttt{HoneycombFreeEnergy.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

