\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Positive strip flux is represented by finite transfer rows and a polynomial vacuum. An exact arch-moment formula becomes a positive homogeneous integral; isolating its smallest coordinate determines the strip exponent. Finite-circumference loop expansions are recalibrated against positive cap amplitudes. Winding-pressure tails and divided-slice enclosing estimates transfer this normalization to strip crossings, concentrating a controlled mass in the specified length window after height averaging.

Local exploration yields a disk transfer representation and an inverse-matrix identity along a rapidity orbit. A boundary deficit and two-gap cylinder contour calculation fix the normalization; two-bridge and winding-polygon bounds then control confinement and mean length. Row matrices construct the balanced puncture polynomial and its boundary asymptotics. Surrounding-polygon localization transports the cylinder observable into finite chord visits, while a marked-tripod comparison converts those visits into the strip bridge mean.

All laws are represented on a common renewal space. Exact-length and logarithmic-window conditioning are combined with recoverable geometric cuts, and controlled changes of measure extend the bounds to thermal and unrestricted ensembles; localized endpoint estimates also yield pulling and correlation-scale exponents. Positive interpolation gives the strip exponent, and a periodic vacuum pairing fixes physical normalization. Controlled-current contour expansions and cylinder barriers produce nesting and unequal-mark bounds; these retain the marked-separation power needed for finite bridge moments.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The strip theorem supplies finite critical masses comparable to R\textasciicircum{}(-1/4) and return-displacement moments. The fixed-corridor first length mass and both R\textasciicircum{}(4/3) mean-length statements are outside it. The finite boundary theorem proves B\_H comparable to H\textasciicircum{}(-1/4), retains mass at least c\_T H\textasciicircum{}(-1/4) inside every sufficiently large fixed transverse corridor TH, and bounds that confined first length mass above by C\_T H\textasciicircum{}(13/12). Together with the unrestricted upper mass bound this settles both mass comparisons and gives a confined normalized-mean upper bound C\_T H\textasciicircum{}(4/3). It does not prove a matching fixed-height first-moment lower bound, either confined or unconfined, nor both R\textasciicircum{}(13/12+o(1)) first moments. The main logarithmic-window lower bound is summed over heights and cannot be substituted for those fixed-height all-length claims. This manuscript has no individually listed Lean scope, so the main Lean flag is null. Critically weighted all-length bridges with a fixed starting port are claimed to have mean length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$ even under confinement, with mass of order $R^{-1/4}$. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. The source proves the unconfined exponent laws and confinement only to width R(log R)\textasciicircum{}2. It does not retain them in every sufficiently wide fixed-aspect corridor. The renewal/change-of-law theorem treats concatenated infinite bridges and finite height laws, with R(log R)\textasciicircum{}2 confinement. It does not supply the target fixed-aspect finite-corridor mass and first-moment comparisons. The source gives finite bridge mass and sharp first-moment/long-fraction estimates in the unconfined bridge law, without the fixed-aspect confined law. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

